WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.590
BRAIN DRAIN:
Historically, big tech has been the go to

00:00:02.590 --> 00:00:07.609
destination for some of the smartest software
engineers, data scientists, and product managers

00:00:07.609 --> 00:00:08.840
in the world.

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:13.460
With bleeding edge projects and top of the
line salaries, it’s no wonder why super

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:18.040
stars are drawn to these companies or at least
they used to be.

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:23.510
It turns out that more and more, super stars
don't want to join big tech companies.

00:00:23.510 --> 00:00:28.630
In fact, just recently Facebook was having
a bit of a hiring crisis where only half of

00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:31.190
top tier job offers were being accepted.

00:00:31.190 --> 00:00:35.829
A large reason for this is that many of these
applicants weren’t actually looking to work

00:00:35.829 --> 00:00:39.900
at Facebook or any other big tech company
at all.

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:44.610
Rather, they simply applied and went through
the interview process in order to get a strong

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:48.180
anchor from which they can negotiate with
the companies that they actually wanted to

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:49.180
join.

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:53.870
It’s not much different with the people
who did end up joining these companies either.

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:57.040
Many of them only see these companies as a
means to an end.

00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:02.030
A way to gain experience, build a strong resume,
and climb the corporate ladder before starting

00:01:02.030 --> 00:01:06.830
their own company or joining a project that
they’re actually passionate about.

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:08.509
This isn’t a new trend either.

00:01:08.509 --> 00:01:13.580
For decades, the startup scene has been dominated
by ex-big tech employees.

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:17.650
But this trend has vastly accelerated over
the past couple years.

00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:19.190
And who could blame them?

00:01:19.190 --> 00:01:24.090
They’re just looking out for themselves
and making the best choices for their careers,

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:29.130
but this brings up the question of: where
does this leave big tech?

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:33.439
As they slowly lose their status as being
the destination for super stars, what will

00:01:33.439 --> 00:01:37.250
happen to their flagship products like the
Metaverse and Google Bard?

00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:41.960
Also, what will happen to the culture of these
companies as they slowly devolve from being

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:46.100
an engineering playground to being just another
corporate office filled with politics, antics,

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:47.530
and egos?

00:01:47.530 --> 00:01:49.980
Well, likely nothing all that great.

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:55.110
So, here’s an in depth look at the big tech
brain drain what this means for these companies

00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:58.770
and the tech industry at large.

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:07.901
THE GREAT MIGRATION:
Starting off with the biggest question: why

00:02:07.901 --> 00:02:11.270
in the world are people not accepting these
job offers?

00:02:11.270 --> 00:02:13.550
Is the compensation just not high enough?

00:02:13.550 --> 00:02:14.690
What’s going on?

00:02:14.690 --> 00:02:20.550
Well, this breaks down into 3 major reasons
starting with big tech offers not being all

00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:22.190
that hard to come by.

00:02:22.190 --> 00:02:27.959
That’s probably quite a controversial statement
because for you or me, scoring a big tech

00:02:27.959 --> 00:02:30.099
offer might be borderline impossible.

00:02:30.099 --> 00:02:36.480
But, for the people who actually end up getting
these offers, it’s often not all that crazy.

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:41.569
In fact, it’s not uncommon for them to apply
at 5-10 companies and get job offers from

00:02:41.569 --> 00:02:43.519
all of them if not most of them.

00:02:43.519 --> 00:02:49.129
If you’re wondering who these people are,
I would recommend checking out teamblind.com.

00:02:49.129 --> 00:02:50.989
Over there, they’re everywhere.

00:02:50.989 --> 00:02:55.890
Literally half the posts on blind are something
along the lines of: Should I take $667 at

00:02:55.890 --> 00:03:02.220
Meta, wait for an L7 offer at Google, or keep
my L7 Amazon job, let me know.

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:06.580
This is especially true once you already have
your foot in at one of these companies.

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:10.459
After that, you don’t even have to apply
to other FAANG companies.

00:03:10.459 --> 00:03:14.739
Instead, recruiters from these other companies
will basically beg you on a weekly basis to

00:03:14.739 --> 00:03:16.299
come interview with them.

00:03:16.299 --> 00:03:20.989
So, the reality is that for a lot of super
stars, once they’re already in the industry

00:03:20.989 --> 00:03:26.659
for 5 or 10 years, scoring another big tech
job offer isn’t some sort of holy grail.

00:03:26.659 --> 00:03:32.130
In fact, they’re often over the whole idea
of working at big tech which brings us into

00:03:32.130 --> 00:03:36.909
reason number 2: the compensation isn’t
enough.

00:03:36.909 --> 00:03:41.629
With slowing growth and high interest rates,
big tech companies have largely cut compensation,

00:03:41.629 --> 00:03:45.370
especially stock comp which is the most lucrative
part of these jobs offers.

00:03:45.370 --> 00:03:50.599
But, that’s not what I’m referring to
when I say that compensation is not enough.

00:03:50.599 --> 00:03:55.560
Rather, what I mean is that higher compensation
alone is not enough to convince super stars

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:56.930
to join.

00:03:56.930 --> 00:04:01.140
The reality is that they’re often already
multi millionaires.

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:04.620
Because of how expensive these cities are,
it’s often the case that they’re not able

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:08.549
to retire even with $2 or $3 million in savings.

00:04:08.549 --> 00:04:13.439
But while, they may not be able to retire,
they’re definitely well off and never need

00:04:13.439 --> 00:04:14.909
to worry about money again.

00:04:14.909 --> 00:04:19.870
As such, they don’t make their next job
decision purely based who is offering the

00:04:19.870 --> 00:04:21.150
biggest salary.

00:04:21.150 --> 00:04:26.060
They’d often much rather take a lower job
offer with a lot more scope and growth opportunities.

00:04:26.060 --> 00:04:30.650
I mean, just think about it, while it might
sound cool from the outside to say that you

00:04:30.650 --> 00:04:35.110
work on the YouTube iOS team, what does that
actually entail?

00:04:35.110 --> 00:04:39.200
It’s not like you’ll be coding out the
new shorts feed or the new recommendation

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:40.820
algorithm from scratch.

00:04:40.820 --> 00:04:46.180
Rather, you’ll probably be working on fixing
bug #2001 regarding community posts or debugging

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:48.410
some issue with dark mode.

00:04:48.410 --> 00:04:50.420
And that’s not Google’s fault.

00:04:50.420 --> 00:04:54.460
That’s simply the reality of joining any
project that’s as mature as YouTube.

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:58.870
Now, I can already hear a bunch of you saying
that you’d love to fix some bugs and earn

00:04:58.870 --> 00:05:03.870
that much, but that’s not exactly how the
high achievers that these companies are targeting

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:04.870
think.

00:05:04.870 --> 00:05:09.040
They’d much rather join a smaller fintech
company or AI company where they can actually

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:13.900
play a larger role in product development
even if the compensation is slightly less.

00:05:13.900 --> 00:05:17.930
And that brings me into the 3rd reason that
superstars are no longer interested in big

00:05:17.930 --> 00:05:20.590
tech which is compensation potential.

00:05:20.590 --> 00:05:25.190
Sure, compensation at smaller companies may
be lower because the cash portion is lower

00:05:25.190 --> 00:05:28.340
but they have far greater growth potential.

00:05:28.340 --> 00:05:32.850
For example, would you rather have $100,000
worth of Google stock and work on Google Bard

00:05:32.850 --> 00:05:37.000
or $100,000 worth of OpenAI stock and work
on ChatGPT?

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:39.510
From that perspective, the answer is obvious.

00:05:39.510 --> 00:05:45.169
So, whether you’re in it for the money or
the role itself, big tech offers simply aren’t

00:05:45.169 --> 00:05:49.910
all that appealing especially when they’re
“not hard to come by”.

00:05:49.910 --> 00:05:55.370
But if super stars are no longer joining big
tech, who is?

00:05:55.370 --> 00:06:04.000
THE OPPORTUNISTS:
Ironically, the question of who is getting

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:09.210
into big tech today is extremely easy to answer
and breaks down into 3 categories starting

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:11.180
with the sell outs.

00:06:11.180 --> 00:06:17.090
The people who have their eye on the ball
for increasing status, job title, and compensation.

00:06:17.090 --> 00:06:20.889
And I want to note that I’m not criticizing
these people by any means.

00:06:20.889 --> 00:06:23.960
They’re just looking out for themselves
as they should be.

00:06:23.960 --> 00:06:28.699
Moreover, these people are more than qualified
for these roles as they’re just as smart

00:06:28.699 --> 00:06:29.850
as the OG superstars.

00:06:29.850 --> 00:06:36.600
That’s why they were able to crush the interviews
and get in but their intentions are far different.

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:40.900
They’re less interested in using their skills
to build the best products, and are more interested

00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:44.080
in using their skills to maximize career progression
and compensation.

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:48.840
A good example of such an employee is quant
engineers.

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:52.910
If you’re not familiar with quant engineers,
their jobs are to use their extensive knowledge

00:06:52.910 --> 00:06:58.370
in applied mathematics, data models, algorithms,
statistics, and calculus to create software

00:06:58.370 --> 00:07:00.780
that can trade the financial markets.

00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:04.820
Essentially, quant engineers are creating
insane algorithms and software just so that

00:07:04.820 --> 00:07:09.629
financial firms can regularly skim 0.5% on
natural gas futures or a few cents on bid

00:07:09.629 --> 00:07:10.949
ask spreads.

00:07:10.949 --> 00:07:16.699
Obviously, rather monotonous soul draining
work but it pays extremely well.

00:07:16.699 --> 00:07:22.350
After all, making 0.5% on a billion dollar
natural gas position adds up to $5 million

00:07:22.350 --> 00:07:24.780
in profit every single trade.

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:29.280
And it seems that FAANG companies are heading
in the exact same direction, especially as

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:32.170
they keep pulling back from moonshot projects.

00:07:32.170 --> 00:07:36.990
Eventually, all that’s left are monotonous
maintenance and refinement jobs who only appeal

00:07:36.990 --> 00:07:43.080
to people are really only in it for the paycheck,
but at least these candidates are highly qualified.

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:49.720
The same cannot be said about our next category
of opportunists which are the game players.

00:07:49.720 --> 00:07:53.909
These people don’t necessarily have strong
engineering backgrounds or a passion for tech.

00:07:53.909 --> 00:07:58.310
They’re often stuck in a corporate job that
pays alright or a even a low paying job that

00:07:58.310 --> 00:08:03.350
they’re stuck in, but what they do have
is a massive amount of drive to change their

00:08:03.350 --> 00:08:04.350
situation.

00:08:04.350 --> 00:08:08.050
And that’s when they hear about all these
people in big tech earning massive salaries

00:08:08.050 --> 00:08:10.750
straight out of college with very little experience.

00:08:10.750 --> 00:08:15.210
Naturally, they decide to make the switch,
but their strategy usually has less to do

00:08:15.210 --> 00:08:19.470
with learning the skills required for the
job and more about learning the skills required

00:08:19.470 --> 00:08:21.600
for the interviews.

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:25.659
You know, grinding out leet code problems,
crafting compelling stores to tell hiring

00:08:25.659 --> 00:08:29.900
managers, networking with as many people as
possible, and just playing the game in general.

00:08:29.900 --> 00:08:35.289
Honestly, this strategy is way more effective
than just being a good engineer.

00:08:35.289 --> 00:08:38.690
This is why a lot of great engineers are never
able to break into FAANG.

00:08:38.690 --> 00:08:41.630
They’re just not able to sell themselves
as well.

00:08:41.630 --> 00:08:46.120
But while this is a phenomenal strategy to
get in the door, the effectiveness of these

00:08:46.120 --> 00:08:49.830
individuals once they get in the door is a
lot more questionable.

00:08:49.830 --> 00:08:54.980
I mean, how much does solving leet code problems
really translate into building highly scalable

00:08:54.980 --> 00:08:58.120
solutions or new innovative products?

00:08:58.120 --> 00:09:03.500
Likely not that much, but atleast, these guys
are largely here by choice which cannot be

00:09:03.500 --> 00:09:07.329
said about our last category of oppurtunists
which are the groomed.

00:09:07.329 --> 00:09:12.690
Now, I don’t want to throw shade at Asians
but this is extremely common within the Asian

00:09:12.690 --> 00:09:13.690
community.

00:09:13.690 --> 00:09:17.900
Basically, starting from age 5, parents will
push them to join a bunch of STEM related

00:09:17.900 --> 00:09:21.570
ECs, coding camps, and take a bunch of CS
classes.

00:09:21.570 --> 00:09:26.500
This way, by the time the kid is 18, regardless
of interest, they’re pretty good at coding.

00:09:26.500 --> 00:09:30.870
From there, they’ll usually attend a top
public university if not an ivy league school

00:09:30.870 --> 00:09:34.510
where they major in CS before they crack their
way into FAANG.

00:09:34.510 --> 00:09:38.860
Putting the whole ethics of all of these aside,
this doesn’t exactly lead to the healthiest

00:09:38.860 --> 00:09:40.269
workforce.

00:09:40.269 --> 00:09:44.930
You basically end up with hundreds of thousands
if not millions of sell outs, game players,

00:09:44.930 --> 00:09:52.240
and groomed individuals across which brings
us into the question of: what happens next?

00:09:52.240 --> 00:10:00.420
THE REPERCUSSIONS:
How exactly each of these groups got here

00:10:00.420 --> 00:10:07.019
is vastly different but they tend to end up
having very similar individualistic motivations.

00:10:07.019 --> 00:10:12.170
Job security, maximum pay, and promotions
which again are not bad goals to have.

00:10:12.170 --> 00:10:15.350
They’re just not all that great for the
company itself.

00:10:15.350 --> 00:10:19.700
This sort of environment is what leads to
entrenched workforces with nothing but office

00:10:19.700 --> 00:10:21.120
politics.

00:10:21.120 --> 00:10:24.910
Much of these employees’ efforts don’t
go into creating the best products or efficiencies

00:10:24.910 --> 00:10:25.910
possible.

00:10:25.910 --> 00:10:30.530
Rather, they go into outperforming their teammates
and pandering to their bosses even if that

00:10:30.530 --> 00:10:33.329
doesn't lead to the best results for the company.

00:10:33.329 --> 00:10:37.820
The reality is that most of these guys don’t
give a hoot in hell about the company other

00:10:37.820 --> 00:10:40.440
than the status that it gives them and the
growing stock price.

00:10:40.440 --> 00:10:41.570
In terms of actually serving their users and
customers, a lot of them couldn’t care less.

00:10:41.570 --> 00:10:45.829
In fact, many of them don’t know how their
role is even linked with the end user.

00:10:45.829 --> 00:10:50.850
Most people would hate such a work environment,
but these guys thrive in this environment.

00:10:50.850 --> 00:10:55.320
Many of them have been dealing with such environments
for their entire careers and by the time they’re

00:10:55.320 --> 00:10:59.500
in their 40s and 50s, they’re masters of
managing politics and getting exactly what

00:10:59.500 --> 00:11:00.839
they want.

00:11:00.839 --> 00:11:04.930
This is how you end up with tech leaders who
have no formal tech background.

00:11:04.930 --> 00:11:09.690
Oftentimes, they’re from the marketing department
or finance department.

00:11:09.690 --> 00:11:15.339
And their playbook is usually just infinitely
cutting costs and increasing efficiencies,

00:11:15.339 --> 00:11:19.209
but this can only take these companies so
far as they veer further and further away

00:11:19.209 --> 00:11:21.250
from what initially made them successful.

00:11:21.250 --> 00:11:27.670
And soon enough, you end up with your next
generation of Intels, Ciscos, and IBMs.

00:11:27.670 --> 00:11:32.329
Meanwhile, the superstars who made FAANG into
what it was move onto up and coming companies

00:11:32.329 --> 00:11:38.230
with promising tech and help scale these companies
to new leagues far beyond what the previous

00:11:38.230 --> 00:11:43.560
generation was able to achieve and the cycle
repeats all over again.

00:11:43.560 --> 00:11:48.100
Not only is FAANG experiencing a brain drain
but they’re also reaching market saturation

00:11:48.100 --> 00:11:49.620
for the first time.

00:11:49.620 --> 00:11:54.709
Check out this video to learn more.

